alse`.
+Each journey selects the offered host cases whose names carry its `<journey>:` prefix, and requires every one of them to live in that journey's runner file list (`authJourneyFiles` in `auth-verification-journeys.ts`). An offered `people:` case in a file the list doesn't name makes every journey refuse before running. So a new people host file needs a driver update first, never a silent omission. `people` runs `organization-upgrade`, `people-factor-enrollment`, `people-passkey-confirmation`, `people-passkey-page`, `people-organizations`, `people-organization-provisioning`, `people-owner-page`, `people-record-faults`, `people-request-limits` and `sign-in-storage`. The storage file runs its d1-workerd and better-sqlite3 legs whichever sign-in host the target names. Each `harness-<receipt>.json` carries `ready` and `readinessEvent` (`proof-bundle-artifacts.ts` `authHarnessReadinessSchema`). The runner refuses any receipt that isn't `ready: true`, and any event the journey doesn't accept: `bootstrap` and `agent` accept `jwks` or `personToken`; `people` also accepts `signInSession`. Readiness only ever strengthens (`personToken` > `jwks` > `signInSession`), so a later callback never replaces an earlier, stronger witness. Each receipt also carries `requestLimits` (`authHarnessRequestLimitsSchema`): its outer counter and the request-limit observations the host drained. Node receipts must say `node-fixed-window`. Cloudflare receipts must say `cloudflare-real-binding`, in every journey: the over-limit cases count on Miniflare's real binding, and a `cloudflare-controlled-binding-stand-in` receipt refuses the run, as does any other counter. `requestLimits.backingFailure` must be `none` everywhere, except exactly one `people` receipt per host: `node-counter-throws` on Node, `cloudflare-binding-rejects` on Cloudflare. The compared target and the printed summary report the counter mix, the 429s by layer (`outer`, `provider`), the unavailable count, the backing-failure kinds and `cloudflareVendorCredit: false`.
 
 Two `people` limits to report, not paper over:
 - Cases that depend on expiries Better Auth computes on its own clock run under controlled time on Node only. Report them by name as unevaluated on `local-workerd`.
